<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byzantine–Bulgarian wars</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Byzantine–Bulgarian wars were a series of conflicts between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the Bulgarian Empire, beginning in the late 5th century and lasting through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparuh</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-iv">Constantine IV</a>, the Byzantines acknowledged the Bulgarian state in 681; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> later inflicted crushing defeats on them, and Simeon I campaigned against them from 893 to 927. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> in 971, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> won the Battle of Kleidion and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1018. A revolt by Theodore Peter and Ivan Asen in 1185 restored Bulgarian rule, and relations fluctuated until the Ottoman Turks took both capitals in 1393 and 1453.</p></div>
